{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_ctp5kb42ztdcbwzxq742wdksdiqf5bs3yctwng4stgtd24lmwika","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## Result: the Rust translation is correct — zero divergences found\n\nThe library is tiny: `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` builds one translation unit (`src/driver.c`) containing `void driver(int x)` and a `static` helper `print_hex`, which dumps the 4 bytes of `x` as `%02x` hex plus a newline.\n\n**Phase A — artifacts** (`translation/SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`)\n- **Symbols:** C exports exactly one symbol, `driver`; Rust exports exactly `driver`. Symbol diff is empty. No module was left untranslated. `print_hex` is `static` in C, so keeping it private in Rust is correct — exporting it would have been the divergence.\n- **Errors:** grepping the C for every rejection construct found **none** — no `return`, no `NULL`/assert/range check, and the public function returns `void`, so there is no channel to report an error. I documented this honestly as one vacuous row rather than inventing rejections, and discharged Phase C via the 11 generic FFI boundary rows an `int`-parameter ABI actually has.\n- **Configs:** no runtime flags, no `#ifdef`s, no `[features]` in `Cargo.toml` — one configuration. The real surface is the byte pattern of the single `int`, giving 18 rows.\n\n**Phases B–D — 25 differential rows, all passing.** Both `.so`s are loaded via `libloading` and driven only through their exported `driver` symbol, so the `#[no_mangle]` wrapper is under test. Since the only observable effect is buffered `printf` output, the harness redirects fd 1 and compares captured bytes — across file-buffered and pipe stdout, interleaved C/Rust calls sharing one `FILE *stdout`, and thousands of seeded-random inputs.\n\n### Two harness defects I found and fixed (worth flagging)\n\n1. **`cargo test` does not rebuild a `cdylib`-only lib target.** My first green run of all 25 tests was actually loading a `.so` built four minutes earlier — it would have passed no matter what the Rust source said. I added an mtime guard that hard-fails with `STALE ARTIFACT`, and all scripts now `cargo build --release` before testing. I verified the guard fires by touching the source without rebuilding.\n2. **libtest's own progress output corrupted the capture.** Under default parallelism, the harness's `test ... ok` lines are written to the real fd 1 from the main thread and landed *inside* my capture window (one failure literally showed `C output = \"FAILED0a000000\\n\"`). Fixed with `harness = false` and a sequential runner, so plain `cargo test` is now correct without `--test-threads=1`. I also made fd-1 restoration RAII, since a panic mid-capture previously left stdout pointed at the temp file.\n\nTo prove the suite isn't vacuous, `mutation_check.sh` injects 8 known bugs into the Rust source (`%2x`, `%02X`, signed-char promotion, `len=3`, `len=5`, missing newline, big-endian order, loop off-by-one) — **all 8 are caught**, 22–24 rows failing each, with the source verified byte-identical afterward.\n\n**One caution:** during an early attempt I ran a shell one-liner whose `cp` backup silently failed on read-only `/tmp` *after* its `sed -i` mutations had already been applied, leaving three live bugs in `translation/src/lib.rs`. I detected this, restored the file, and confirmed via `grep` that lines 45–63 match the original exactly. `c_src/` was never modified. The lesson is baked into `mutation_check.sh`, which now uses `$TMPDIR`, a `trap`-based restore, and a `cmp` verification.\n\nRun everything with `cd translation && bash run_all.sh` (add `OFFLINE=` if a registry is reachable — `libloading` resolved from the local cache here, so `--offline` is the default)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1037,"cache_read_input_tokens":77719,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1037,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"10d4e32d-f1fe-46c3-8899-4dcab44cf8f7","uuid":"e8b41143-8488-4058-a987-73c7cc845af1","timestamp":"2026-08-28T16:00:21.499Z"}
